editor.nod_help.full.intro=<h2>What is NOD?</h2><p><b>NOD</b> is the text format Syscalculator uses to describe a converter. A NOD file tells the program the converter name, visible input and output labels, symbols near values, and the rules that transform the value.</p><h2>The basic idea</h2><p>A NOD file reads like a small recipe. First comes the name, then labels, optional symbols and introduction text, then the rules for calculation or text conversion. The last line is usually <a class="cmd-link" href="nodpage:cmd:end"><code>end</code></a>.</p><h2>Why plain text?</h2><p>Because the knowledge stays visible. A Celsius/Fahrenheit rule, a euro rate or an old phone renumbering rule is not hidden in program code; it remains readable in the file.</p><h2>Mini example</h2><pre>Name Celsius to Fahrenheit&#13;&#10;input1 Celsius&#13;&#10;input2 Fahrenheit&#13;&#10;format ##.00&#13;&#10;math ans * 1.8&#13;&#10;math ans + 32&#13;&#10;end</pre><p>Read this from top to bottom: the converter gets a name, shows two labels, chooses number formatting and runs two calculation steps with <code>ans</code> as the current value.</p>
editor.nod_help.full.guide=<p>This chapter is about working in the editor window. You write NOD text in the middle, use the toolbar for opening, saving, testing and validation, and read command tips when the caret is on a known line.</p><h2>The window</h2>{NodEditorScreenshot}<p class="shot-caption">Example: a Celsius/Fahrenheit converter with the command tip for <code>Name</code>.</p><h2>Create a converter</h2><ol><li>Start with <a class="cmd-link" href="nodpage:cmd:name"><code>Name</code></a>, <a class="cmd-link" href="nodpage:cmd:input1"><code>input1</code></a> and <a class="cmd-link" href="nodpage:cmd:input2"><code>input2</code></a>.</li><li>Optionally add <a class="cmd-link" href="nodpage:cmd:symb1"><code>Symb1</code></a> through <a class="cmd-link" href="nodpage:cmd:symb4"><code>Symb4</code></a> for symbols left or right of values.</li><li>Write the conversion with <a class="cmd-link" href="nodpage:cmd:math"><code>math</code></a>, <a class="cmd-link" href="nodpage:cmd:trans"><code>trans</code></a> or <a class="cmd-link" href="nodpage:cmd:chg"><code>chg</code></a>.</li><li>Use <b>Validate</b> to find syntax errors and <b>Test</b> to try the result.</li><li>Save the file when the converter is correct.</li></ol><h2>Command tips</h2><p>The blue balloon gives short help for the line where the caret is placed. Sometimes it also shows a more modern form. Use <b>Replace</b> only when you really want that modern form; <b>More help</b> opens the full command page.</p><h2>Undo, redo and restore</h2><p><b>Undo</b> and <b>Redo</b> are about recent text edits. <b>Restore</b> is larger: it returns the current tab to the last opened or saved version. <b>Tools &gt; Repair lines</b> is different; it tries to repair pasted or merged NOD lines.</p>
editor.nod_help.guide.intro=This chapter is about working in the editor window. You write NOD text in the middle, use the toolbar for opening, saving, testing and validation, and read command tips when the caret is on a known line.
editor.nod_help.guide.window_title=The window
